Methods (Inoculant is its own C++ code; no code was copied from the projects below, only their published ideas):
  PGD - Madry et al., "Towards Deep Learning Models Resistant to Adversarial Attacks" (2018)
  MI-FGSM - Dong et al., "Boosting Adversarial Attacks with Momentum" (2018)
  EOT - Athalye et al., "Synthesizing Robust Adversarial Examples" (2018)
  Feature squeezing - Xu et al., "Feature Squeezing: Detecting Adversarial Examples in Deep Neural Networks" (2018)
  JPEG / spatial smoothing / Gaussian augmentation defences - as collected in IBM's Adversarial Robustness Toolbox
      (https://github.com/Trusted-AI/adversarial-robustness-toolbox, MIT)
  Disrupting image-translation deepfakes with adversarial perturbations - Ruiz et al. (2020),
      https://github.com/natanielruiz/disrupting-deepfakes (no licence given: the idea only, nothing of its code)
  Detecting and removing Nightshade-style poison - NightshadeAntidote by Richard Aragon
      (https://github.com/RichardAragon/NightshadeAntidote, MIT)
  Identity cloaking against face recognition - Fawkes (Shan et al., 2020), LowKey (Cherepanova et al., 2021)

Planned modules (not in this version) and their licences, to keep in mind: shieldfont (isaqueseneda, AGPL-3.0) and
miasma (austin-weeks, GPL-3.0) can't be copied into an MIT project: Inoculant's text tools will be written fresh from
their ideas, or run them as separate programs. OWASP Agent Memory Guard: Apache-2.0.

The model is NOT part of Inoculant. ArcFace w600k_r50 is fetched on first use from FaceFusion's model releases
(https://github.com/facefusion/facefusion-assets/releases) if it isn't on the PC already; so are the face detector
and landmarkers (RetinaFace/SCRFD det_10g, 2DFAN4, FAN 68/5):
  det_10g and w600k_r50 - InsightFace (https://github.com/deepinsight/insightface): the code is MIT; the pretrained
      models are for NON-COMMERCIAL research use
  2DFAN4 - Adrian Bulat (https://github.com/1adrianb/face-alignment): BSD-3-Clause
  FAN 68/5 - FaceFusion
Inoculant writes its own "gradient graph" from w600k_r50 on the PC it runs on (w600k_r50_grad.onnx: the same
weights, run backwards as well); it keeps the model's licence and isn't shipped.

The face detection and alignment code is shared with Mimic (Cursed Entertainment), which follows FaceFusion
(https://github.com/facefusion/facefusion, Henry Ruhs and contributors).

Runs on ONNX Runtime (Microsoft, MIT) with DirectML (Microsoft, MIT). Window: SDL2 (zlib), Dear ImGui (MIT);
pictures: stb (MIT / public domain).

Use it on your own pictures, or on pictures of people who agreed. It is a protective tool: don't use it to hide
or forge anyone else's identity.
